POTS Hyperadrenergic Phenotype
Complex Fatigue Entities
Sympathetic overactivity upon standing

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-037
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of acute tachycardia, severe tremor, and presyncope to rule out cardiogenic arrhythmia or thyroid storm.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden, uncontrollable racing heart, severe full-body shaking, and an inability to stand without feeling faint. Immediate in-person assessment was mandated to rule out atrial fibrillation, supraventricular tachycardia (SVT), or acute hypertensive crisis, and to stabilize the severe autonomic distress prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My heart is pounding out of my chest, I can't stop shaking, and if I stand up I feel like I am going to have a stroke.
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an explosive 48-hour exacerbation of dysautonomia. The patient reports severe symptoms triggered strictly by assuming an upright posture. The sequence involves immediate onset of profound anxiety, a rapid and bounding tachycardia, and a severe generalized tremor. The patient notes an elevated standing blood pressure accompanied by an intense sensation of adrenaline overload. Profound post-exertional fatigue follows these episodes.
Functional Status: Bedbound; actively avoiding any movement that requires elevating the head above the heart.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; immense psychological distress due to the physical sensation of an impending cardiac event. High fall risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/80 supine, 155/100 standing mmHg | HR: 75 supine, 140 standing bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 20 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Visibly tremulous, flushed, anxious, exhibiting tachypnea secondary to sympathetic surge.
Physical Exam: Postural vital signs demonstrate archetypal sympathetic overactivity upon standing: an excessive increase in heart rate combined with a marked hypertensive surge. Cardiovascular exam reveals regular rhythm; no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Neurological exam reveals a fine, fast postural tremor, brisk reflexes bilaterally, and intact cranial nerves. Thyroid is not palpably enlarged.
Environmental Observations: Room is quiet. Patient is lying completely flat, clutching the bedsheets.
Medication Reconciliation: No illicit substance use. No recent changes to caffeine intake. No sympathomimetic decongestants taken.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: POTS Hyperadrenergic Phenotype - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Supraventricular Tachycardia (SVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tachycardia is entirely dependent on posture (resolving when supine) rather than a sudden-onset, sudden-offset paroxysmal arrhythmia at rest.

Pheochromocytoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symptoms are strictly orthostatic rather than spontaneous episodic attacks, though formal endocrinology workup may be considered if symptoms remain refractory.

Panic Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear, measurable, and reproducible orthostatic hypertensive and tachycardic response, though secondary anxiety is understandably present.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of extreme tachycardia, hypertension, and tremor mimicking a critical cardiac or endocrine emergency required an immediate, on-site face-to-face evaluation. Objectively establishing that the severe hemodynamic shifts were purely orthostatic allowed the exclusion of primary cardiac arrhythmias (e.g., atrial fibrillation, SVT) and prevented a highly traumatic and unneeded emergency department admission. The severity of the sympathetic surge necessitated urgent pharmacological blunting in the home to restore functional safety and alleviate severe distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a single low dose of oral Diazepam 2.5mg to acutely blunt the massive sympathetic/adrenaline surge. Advised strict horizontal rest. Targeted intervention strategy includes Low Dose Naltrexone, Magnesium L-Threonate, and B6 to be initiated following specialist confirmation.
Positive Behavioral Change: By systematically clenching your leg and core muscles for 10 seconds before attempting to stand, you will actively compress your blood vessels, directly preventing the sudden blood pressure drop that triggers the massive adrenaline surge, rewarding yourself with a calmer, steadier heart rate.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Blood pressure monitor recommended for controlled daily tracking.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 2-3 days to initiate central sympatholytic agents (e.g., Clonidine or targeted Beta-blockers).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if the heart rate remains above 130 bpm while lying completely flat for 20 minutes, if severe crushing chest pain develops, or if actual fainting occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This condition represents a severe misfiring of the autonomic nervous system's fight-or-flight response; it requires careful, targeted medication to stabilize.
Clinical Course: The acute adrenaline surge will subside with rest and medication. You must avoid sudden postural changes to prevent immediately re-triggering the sympathetic response.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if your heart will not slow down even after lying flat for 20 minutes, or if you feel a heavy, crushing pain in the center of your chest.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your nervous system is mistakenly dumping massive amounts of adrenaline into your bloodstream every time you stand up; the temporary medication acts as a chemical brake to calm the nervous system down.
Actionable Steps: Eliminate all caffeine and sugary drinks completely tomorrow; removing these chemical stimulants prevents unnecessary acceleration of your nervous system, directly reinforcing a lower baseline heart rate and less tremor.

